[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding StrictMode and Unnecessary Stubbing Exceptions in Mockito"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mockito is the most popular mocking framework for Java, and since version 2.1.0, it introduced StrictMode by default. This change helps developers write cleaner, more maintainable unit tests by failing fast when stubbing goes wrong. In this tutorial, we will explore StrictMode and how to handle UnnecessaryStubbingException."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is StrictMode in Mockito?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"StrictMode in Mockito aims to reduce boilerplate code, detect potential bugs, and enforce cleaner test design. When a mock is created using either the @Mock annotation combined with MockitoExtension or via Mockito.strict(), Mockito tracks how stubs are used throughout the test execution."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"The Cause of UnnecessaryStubbingException"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"An UnnecessaryStubbingException is thrown when you define a stubbing rule on a mock, but the method is never actually invoked during the execution of the test. Mockito considers this 'dead code' or 'redundant configuration', which can sometimes indicate a mismatch between the test setup and the code under test."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
public class UserServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @InjectMocks
    private UserService userService;

    @Test
    public void testFindUser() {
        // This stubbing is defined...
        when(userRepository.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(new User()));

        // But the method is never called in userService.doSomethingElse(),
        // resulting in UnnecessaryStubbingException!
        userService.doSomethingElse();
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"How to Fix UnnecessaryStubbingException"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Remove the unused stubbing if it is no longer required for the test scenario."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure the code under test actually invokes the stubbed method with the correct parameters."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Move common stubbing configurations to a @BeforeEach setup method only if they are used across all tests."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Bypassing Strictness Locally"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"In rare cases, you might want to disable strictness for a specific test or mock. You can achieve this by using the @MockitoSettings annotation with Strictness.LENIENT, or by using Mockito.lenient() on a specific stubbing."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"@Test
@MockitoSettings(strictness = Strictness.LENIENT)
public class LenientUserServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @InjectMocks
    private UserService userService;

    @Test
    public void testWithLenientStubbing() {
        // This will not throw UnnecessaryStubbingException even if unused
        lenient().when(userRepository.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(new User()));

        userService.doSomethingElse();
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"StrictMode and UnnecessaryStubbingException are powerful tools designed to keep your test suite lean and reliable. While it might be tempting to use lenient() everywhere, resolving unused stubs properly leads to better-designed tests and fewer hidden bugs in your application code."}]
